1
00:00:01,010 --> 00:00:04,324
Running a tournament shouldn't be harder than winning one.

2
00:00:04,406 --> 00:00:08,303
Yet most organizers still juggle spreadsheets and group chats:

3
00:00:08,343 --> 00:00:12,755
drawing brackets by hand, chasing entry fees, and arguing about match times.

4
00:00:12,795 --> 00:00:15,665
And players often wait weeks for their prize money.

5
00:00:16,295 --> 00:00:20,935
Meet TurniRUM: an open-source tournament platform where AI does the organizing,

6
00:00:20,975 --> 00:00:22,791
and PayPal handles the money.

7
00:00:22,931 --> 00:00:23,974
You run the event.

8
00:00:24,014 --> 00:00:27,244
The bracket, the schedule and the prizes take care of themselves.

9
00:00:27,674 --> 00:00:28,870
Here's how it works.

10
00:00:28,910 --> 00:00:33,500
The web app runs on Supabase: a Postgres database protected by row-level security,

11
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:36,205
and an Edge Function with all the business logic.

12
00:00:36,259 --> 00:00:41,010
It asks Google Gemini to plan, uses PayPal for payments and payouts,

13
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:44,891
and Brevo for email, while a scheduled job sends the reminders.

14
00:00:45,921 --> 00:00:47,159
Let's see it in action.

15
00:00:47,552 --> 00:00:51,787
An organizer simply describes the event: a Valorant cup for sixteen teams,

16
00:00:51,827 --> 00:00:54,527
twenty dollars entry, prizes for the top three.

17
00:00:55,309 --> 00:00:57,061
Gemini fills in the whole form,

18
00:00:57,101 --> 00:01:01,129
from the rules and the schedule to the prize split and the bracket format.

19
00:01:01,986 --> 00:01:05,556
The organizer confirms the ten percent platform fee, and publishes.

20
00:01:06,686 --> 00:01:10,126
Players register their team and pay the entry fee with PayPal,

21
00:01:10,166 --> 00:01:12,326
using an account or a card.

22
00:01:12,949 --> 00:01:15,822
Here we're in the PayPal sandbox, with a test card.

23
00:01:16,519 --> 00:01:19,359
The server creates the order with the price from the database,

24
00:01:19,399 --> 00:01:23,960
captures it, and admits the team only after PayPal confirms the payment.

25
00:01:24,088 --> 00:01:26,124
Card details never touch our servers.

26
00:01:27,254 --> 00:01:31,574
When registration closes, one click lets the AI choose the format,

27
00:01:31,614 --> 00:01:35,365
seed the teams by their past results, and plan the schedule.

28
00:01:36,006 --> 00:01:39,220
The bracket is ready, and every captain is notified.

29
00:01:40,050 --> 00:01:41,293
Can't make your match?

30
00:01:41,513 --> 00:01:43,559
When both captains press Reschedule,

31
00:01:43,599 --> 00:01:46,719
the AI moves it to a free slot with no clashes.

32
00:01:46,932 --> 00:01:50,387
And reminders go out by email, a day and an hour before.

33
00:01:51,117 --> 00:01:52,245
Need teammates?

34
00:01:52,296 --> 00:01:54,395
The AI studies the tournaments you've played,

35
00:01:54,435 --> 00:01:57,878
and suggests players who fit, explaining every pick.

36
00:01:59,208 --> 00:02:02,315
Results update the bracket and the standings live.

37
00:02:03,134 --> 00:02:05,034
When the organizer finishes the tournament,

38
00:02:05,074 --> 00:02:07,226
TurniRUM settles the money automatically.

39
00:02:08,217 --> 00:02:11,787
Prizes go straight to the winners' PayPal accounts through PayPal Payouts,

40
00:02:11,827 --> 00:02:15,721
ten percent goes to the platform, and the organizer withdraws the rest.

41
00:02:16,751 --> 00:02:21,878
So Gemini works in seven places: drafting, formats, seeding,

42
00:02:21,918 --> 00:02:25,124
scheduling, rescheduling, reminders and teammate matching.

43
00:02:25,164 --> 00:02:29,807
And there's always a safe fallback when a model is busy.

44
00:02:30,537 --> 00:02:33,372
Organizers save hours and get paid fairly.

45
00:02:33,444 --> 00:02:37,506
Players get clear brackets, fair schedules, and prizes paid automatically.

46
00:02:37,546 --> 00:02:42,047
And clubs and communities can finally run paid events with full transparency.

47
00:02:42,577 --> 00:02:43,494
TurniRUM.

48
00:02:43,535 --> 00:02:45,339
Tournaments that run themselves.

49
00:02:45,379 --> 00:02:47,239
Open source, on GitHub.
